          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "**Adherence** means sticking to the treatment plan agreed with a clinician. In diabetes, that includes taking glucose-lowering medication, monitoring, and attending reviews. Diabetes is largely self-managed, since most decisions that affect the outcome are made by the person between appointments. The guidance notes that adherence to glucose-lowering medication is measurably imperfect in practice (*Sticking with a diabetes regimen*, National diabetes association and self-management education guidance).\n\nThe guidance says that missed doses are often not a matter of willpower. Cost, complicated routines, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting account for most of it. Each has a different fix, so a clinician can help find the specific barrier rather than assume the problem is personal.\n\nStructured diabetes self-management education is described as a core part of care. It is offered at diagnosis, yearly if targets are not being met, when complications develop, and at care transitions. It improves glucose control and quality of life, but it is often underused (same source).\n\nMy search did not find a passage that quantifies how much poorer adherence changes diabetes outcomes, so I can't give you specific figures from this material. A clinician can explain what missed doses mean for an individual's blood sugar and long-term risk. If you have trouble keeping to a plan, that is a good thing to raise at your next appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
